‐ 
Review question 
We reviewed the evidence on interventions to promote safe, effective and appropriate medicines use by healthcare consumers. 
Background 
Medicines use is a key area of healthcare, with many people using medicines for a wide range of conditions. However, many people do not use medicines appropriately, and this can lead to harm to individuals and to society. 
Interventions to improve medicines‐use by healthcare consumers are often designed to promote behaviour change, reduce harm and improve clinical outcomes. These interventions may be delivered by healthcare professionals, or by other organisations, such as pharmacies, or by individuals. 
We included systematic reviews that assessed interventions to improve medicines consumption, use and use‐related outcomes, including adherence, knowledge, clinical outcomes, medicines problems, adverse events, costs and mortality. 
Study characteristics 
We included 100 reviews of interventions that were published on the databases of Cochrane and the Database for Reviews of Effects (DRE). We included reviews that assessed a wide range and varied interventions, including those that targeted healthcare consumers to improve medicines self‐management, medicines use and medicines problems. 
We found 75 relevant reviews, which included 12,800 participants. The reviews were conducted in 32 countries and included a wide range, but not all, of the healthcare systems. 
Key results 
We found that interventions to improve healthcare consumers' medicines consumption, adherence and use‐problems, and to reduce adverse events and mortality, appear generally effective. 
Intervention types that appear to be effective include: 
• simplified dosing regimes (with positive, although mixed, effects);
• interventions involving pharmacologists in medicines management (with positive and mixed effects);
• pharmaceutical care services;
• education delivered with or without self‐management training, counselling or enhanced follow up;
• information and counselling;
• financial incentives;
• organisational interventions;
•

of ICU survivors 
The intensive care stay has been associated with a number physical and psychological consequences, known collectively post‐intensively‐care syndrome (PACS). These consequences may be temporary or long‐lasting, and may include physical symptoms such as fatigue, pain, and disturbed sleep, and psychological symptoms such as depression, anxiety, and post‐trauma stress disorder (PSTD). 
PACS is a complex condition that may be difficult to treat. It is important to identify and treat the unmet needs of ICU patients, as these may be related to their physical and psychological health. 
There are many different ways to provide follow‐up care for ICU survivors. These include: 
• home visits by a nurse or other healthcare professional; 
• telephone calls; 
• online services; 
• group sessions; 
• individual sessions; 
and 
• specialist support. 
The aim of this review was to assess whether follow‐ups services are effective for identifying and addressing unmet health care needs of ICU survivor. 
We searched for studies that compared follow‐up with no follow‐ups or standard care, and included adults who had been admitted to the ICU and discharged from hospital. 
We found five studies (4 randomised studies, 1 non‐randomized study) with a total of participants. 
We assessed the certainty of evidence and found that the evidence was of low to very low certainty. 
We were unable to assess our secondary objective because we found too few studies to justify a subgroup analysis.
